  "query_preview": "..\n(Saturday through Wednesday)\nIssued at 1204 AM CDT Thu Oct 29 2026\n\nAfter a quiet Saturday, global models show additional rain chances\nSunday night into Monday as another deep trough approaches from \nthe west. This system is projected to have another strong push of \nmoisture from the south being advected to the region, which spells\n30-60% rain probabilities for a majority of the CWA. However, \nthere does appear to be greater uncertainty with this system, as \nmodels disagree on a few synoptic features. These disagreements \nprimarily hinge on differing track and timing of the trough, and \npotential for a dry slot to infiltrate the region before best lift\narrives like the GFS currently shows. Regardless, it is likely \nthat there will be at least a brief window for showers and storms \nSunday-Sunday night. \n \nWe can anticipate a strong cold front to charge over the Panhandles \nMonday as stout northwest flow aloft on the backside of the latter \ntrough passage stretches over the region. Quick pressure rises and \nstacked jet dynamics behind this FROPA spells a solidly windy start \nto the week Mon & Tue, with sustained northwest winds of 20-30 mph \ngusting 40 mph or possibly greater both days (10-40% chance for \ngusts >50 mph). This will aid cold air advection to the southern \nHigh Plains, with below average temperatures to follow on Tue and \nWed. Recent trends from global models have kept sufficient moisture \npooled behind the front Mon night into Tuesday, which could help \ngenerate additional showers. If cloud cover and rain are as \nprevalent as this data is suggesting, stronger wind gusts may become \nless common or less likely altogether. Model agreement on synoptic \nevolution further fractures beyond Tuesday, but it is becoming more \napparent that our active pattern will likely come to a halt.\n\nHarrel",
